evidence of how close the world has come  to a nuclear disaster in ukraine  shelling caused this hole on the roof of  a building that houses nuclear fuel and  radioactive waste  here more damage at europe's biggest  nuclear plant seized by russian forces  in march and placed on the front line of  a war zone  a team of international inspectors  braved the dangers to visit the site  last week  the head of the un's nuclear watchdog  led the mission and he wants the world  to know what it found  the hits that this facility has uh  received and that i could personally see  and assess  together with my experts is simply  um  unacceptable we are playing with fire  and something very very catastrophic  could take place he released a report on  how to stop the war in ukraine from  triggering a nuclear catastrophe  a key recommendation was that shelling  on the site and in its vicinity should  be stopped immediately  this requires agreement by all relevant  parties to the establishment of a  nuclear safety and security protection  zone around the zaparisian nuclear plant  the inspectors didn't apportion blame  for the damage suffered so far here but  their report noted the presence of  russian military vehicles such as this  one  it said any vehicles that could  interfere with the safe running of the  plant should be removed  sustaining the supply of electricity  powering the plant was also flagged as  vital  it's been cut off a number of times  raising concerns of a nuclear meltdown  the mayor of the town where the plant is  based fled the area a few months ago  speaking from a nearby city he accused  russia of attacking the facility to  pressure ukraine into pulling back from  an offense of its launch against russian  positions in the south  russia has blamed ukraine for being the  one endangering the facility  but it seems unlikely russian forces  will comply with the report's  recommendations to stop shelling and  effectively withdraw  debra haynes sky news in southern  ukraine
 

